{
  "abstract": "How much of the dependence between grid directions can be recovered by conservation laws? We study three explicit comparison laws for capped supports, where each row and column contains at most two occupied points. For every fixed density $0<c\\le2$, scalar pair conservation changes the logarithm of an independent-direction void probability by at most $O_c(\\log^2 n)$ below and $\\log n+O_c(1)$ above. Labelled per-point partner conservation has correction $O_c(n)$, after a common vector normalization cancels. We then construct antipodal direction switches preserving every pair identity, all pointwise direction degrees, the axis graph, and each class's clique profile. Admissible switches form a binary kernel generated by original collinear triangles; geometric realizations are precisely cuts. For an explicit centrally symmetric signed-configuration source, the incidence rank has expectation $3c^3n\\log n/(2\\pi^2)+O_c(n)$. Cleaning and thinning retain this estimate at exact size $2\\lfloor cn/2\\rfloor$ for $0<c<2$. Rare two-line sources give $\\log\\mathbb E e^{\\beta\\rho}=\\beta c^2n^2/8+O_{\\beta,c}(n\\log n)$ for each fixed $\\beta>0$. Geometric conditioning therefore produces a quadratic negative no-three penalty and selects almost maximal rank, despite preserving the source's single-class marginals before conditioning. We derive the inverse source tilt needed to recover the physical law and identify its uncontrolled negative exponential moment. Complete proofs and exact rational and binary controls support the results. These comparisons neither estimate the true-grid dependence correction nor construct an unbounded no-three family above $1.8n$.",
